### Before you register the privileged runner

> **Register it as a PROJECT runner locked to this repository. Never an instance
> runner; never a group runner over a group containing untrusted projects.**

The registration *scope* is the security boundary, not the tag: tags are
cooperative routing, and any project on the instance can write
`tags: [{{ ci_runner_tag | default('infrastructure') }}]` in its own
`.gitlab-ci.yml`. Registered instance-wide, that one line gets an arbitrary
authenticated user root + Docker-in-Docker execution on this cluster, on a runner
that also holds SSH reach to the hosts. Nothing in this repository can check the
scope, so it is verified by hand.

Verify after registering, and after any re-registration or token rotation:

```bash
# Expect "project_type"
curl -sH "PRIVATE-TOKEN: $TOKEN" "https://{{ git_host }}/api/v4/runners/<id>" | jq .runner_type
```

`gitlab-runner-privileged/README.md` has the full reasoning, the sizing model and
the tenant-build alternative.
{%- endif %}

## House style for a new module

- A dedicated namespace with Pod Security Standards labels: `enforce` at the
  strictest level the workload actually tolerates, `warn`/`audit` at
  `restricted` so drift is visible without breaking admission.
- `components/netpol-baseline` for default-deny ingress, plus the shared egress
  components (`netpol-egress-dns`, `netpol-egress-public`,
  `netpol-egress-apiserver`) rather than hand-written copies. Adding ANY egress
  policy makes egress default-deny for the pods it selects — enumerate every
  path the workload needs. The ingress deny is not optional:
  `scripts/check-default-deny-coverage.py` fails `task flux:lint` for any
  namespace that owns a workload without one, and the only declared exemption is
  `flux-system`, whose gotk manifests carry their own policies.
- A VPA (`Initial` for single-replica apps, `Off` for stateful ones you tune by
  hand, and never CPU-controlled where an HPA owns CPU).
- Its own ServiceMonitor/PodMonitor and PrometheusRule if it has metrics —
  Prometheus discovers both in every namespace, so nothing needs editing in the
  observability stage.
- No literal domains, IPs or CIDRs: use the `${cluster_*}` substitution keys.
